/home/sat/mcrl-v025-rank-ws/STATIC-BASELINE-FAMILY-2026-09-10.md:103:- `FIRST_IMPROVEMENT_FP` remains the designated strong classical-search comparator and is not a required learned win target, as stipulated. It is **not an empirical numerical upper bound on this measured family**: `MYOPIC_GREEDY` and `RSS_MAX` are higher. Calling 13.430253 a universal ceiling after this run would contradict the verified table.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-rank-ws/STATIC-BASELINE-FAMILY-2026-09-10.md:105:- `RANDOM` remains the sanity reference. `ROUND_ROBIN` is 69.37% below it and must be reported. The diversity evidence does not support calling this particular failure crowding: it has the lowest possible measured `modal_frac` (0.01) and exactly 100 active beams at every anchor. Its poor result instead coincides with over-dispersion, 219,036 J (the largest denominator), only 798/1200 served, and just 48/1200 attaining target. Thus “below random implies crowding” is not valid without the occupancy diagnostics; this arm fails for a different static pathology.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-rank-ws/STATIC-BASELINE-FAMILY-2026-09-10.md:107:- `RSS_MAX`'s high EE is not an artefact of dropping users: it serves 1200/1200, attains 297/1200, has the largest pooled numerator, and the smallest pooled energy denominator. Under declared full-buffer/no-demand-cap scoring, it is the strongest measured arm despite being demand-blind.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-rank-ws/STATIC-BASELINE-FAMILY-2026-09-10.md:109:- `MYOPIC_GREEDY` is a single nominal sweep, whereas the fixed point selects strict moves on realised boundary 0 from a carrier-conditioned start. Their ordering therefore does not show a certification error or make either algorithm a global optimizer. It shows that the local fixed-point construction is start-, information-, and traversal-dependent.
